  "articleBody": "When a Texas storm hits and leaves your roof looking less than its best, knowing what to do immediately can feel overwhelming. But taking the right steps to document the damage is super important for your peace of mind and for any potential insurance claims. It’s all about creating a clear record of what happened and when.\n\n### What to Prepare Before You Start\n\nFirst things first: prioritize safety and prevent more water from getting in without destroying evidence. Don't climb onto a damaged roof yourself – it's just not safe. Once immediate risks are managed, gather the following documentation:\n\n*   **Dated Photographs:** Take wide shots of your whole home and close-ups of specific damaged areas. Get pictures from different angles. Make sure the date and time are clearly visible on these photos if possible. This helps establish the timing of the damage.\n*   **Interior Damage:** If water came through, document any interior damage too. Stains on ceilings, walls, or damaged personal items should all be photographed.\n*   **Storm Date & Details:** Note down the exact date and time the storm occurred. Any official weather reports for your area can also be helpful context.\n*   **Roof Materials & Prior Condition:** If you have any records of your roof's age, material type, or past repairs, gather those. This helps show the condition of the roof before the storm.\n*   **Emergency Repair Receipts:** If you had to make any immediate temporary repairs to prevent further damage (like tarping), keep those receipts. However, avoid making permanent repairs before an official assessment.\n\nHaving this information ready makes the process smoother when you contact your insurer or a professional for a damage assessment. Discounted Roofing Co. specializes in helping homeowners navigate these exact situations. We can help you identify visible and less obvious signs of damage.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-03"
}